<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- The old brand_* splash palette (light grey + teal) died with the dead
         WebView layout it styled: MainActivity has been pure Compose since the
         native rewrite and never inflated activity_main.xml, so those colours
         could only ever describe a screen no user has seen. -->
    <!-- PREVIEW-WINDOW FALLBACK, not a value that matches every theme.
         This comment used to claim the resource "MUST match ui/theme/Color.kt's
         SurfaceApp". Since the five-theme switch there is no single SurfaceApp
         (Day #EAEEF3, Sand #EFE8DC, Calm #0F1319, Night #070B12, Dusk #13111C),
         so that invariant was unsatisfiable by construction and a Day or Sand
         user got a near-black frame on cold start.

         What it actually is: Calm's surfaceApp (this app's original and
         still-default palette) serving the system's starting/preview window,
         which the framework draws from the manifest theme BEFORE this process
         draws anything. From window attach onwards MainActivity repaints the
         ground with the palette AppTheme.load() restored, so the mismatch is
         bounded to that one framework-drawn frame. Removing it entirely needs
         per-palette launch themes.

         The dead ic_launcher_background colour that sat below is gone: nothing
         referenced it (both mipmap-anydpi-v26 adaptive icons point at
         @drawable/ic_launcher_background, a different resource whose own header
         records the replacement), the name collided exactly with that live
         drawable, and its #1A1A1A was neither a brand nor a token colour. The
         real launcher ground is the drawable's own dark gradient, pinned by
         BrandMarkWiringContractTest. -->
    <color name="window_background">#FF0F1319</color>
</resources>
